LDPlayer environment fix (if it ever breaks again)

Symptom chain we hit: LDPlayer stuck at 94% engine-starting → black main window that crashes on interaction → Diagnostics panel showing "Hyper-V: Not disabled" even after unchecking Hyper-V/Virtual Machine Platform/Windows Hypervisor Platform in "Turn Windows features on or off," and even after using LDPlayer's own "Fix now" button.

Root cause: Windows 11 (25H2 on this machine) keeps Virtualization-Based Security (VBS) running via a UEFI lock, independent of the Hyper-V feature checkbox and Memory Integrity toggle. Confirm via msinfo32 → System Summary → "Virtualization-based security" (shows "Running" when this is the problem).

Fix that actually worked (checkbox toggles and LDPlayer's "Fix now" alone did NOT stick):

# Run from an elevated Command Prompt or PowerShell (Ctrl+Shift+Enter after
# typing cmd in Start search, since right-click "Run as administrator"
# wasn't available in the Start menu on this machine)
bcdedit /set hypervisorlaunchtype off
reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard" /v EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f
reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\DeviceGuard\Scenarios\HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity" /v Enabled /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f

Then restart. Recheck msinfo32 — may need a second restart for the UEFI lock to fully release. Once VBS shows disabled, LDPlayer loads and renders normally.

Reversible any time: flip Memory Integrity back on in Windows Security, or re-run the same reg add commands with /d 1 and bcdedit /set hypervisorlaunchtype auto, then restart.

Machine specifics: Windows 11 64-bit 25H2, Ryzen 7 9800X3D, RTX 5080, LDPlayer 9.5.31.0, install path C:\LDPlayer\LDPlayer9\.

