Card-in-hand ID — status: working, approach validated, now with semi-automated data collection

Files: config/hand_region.py (calibrated pixel geometry of the 4-card hand row), perception/card_detector.py (matching logic + template storage), perception/label_hand.py (adds a new card to the template library from the current test_screenshot.png), perception/debug_card_match.py (saves a visual side-by-side of crop vs matched template — use this whenever results look off, don't guess blindly).

Important design decision, arrived at after a real failed attempt: first tried matching hand-slot crops against Supercell's official card catalog icon art (downloaded via perception/download_card_icons.py from the iconUrls already present in config/cards.json). This performed badly — all matches under 0.17 confidence, 3 of 4 wrong. Root cause, confirmed visually via debug_card_match.py: the catalog icons use different framing/zoom than the live hand-slot render, and don't include the card border + elixir-cost badge the hand UI overlays. Fix: abandoned catalog icons as the matching source entirely; templates are now built from real hand-slot screenshots via label_hand.py instead, so comparisons are pixel-consistent (same framing on both sides). This worked immediately — 1.000 confidence on a self-match, and critically, 0.98–0.999 confidence when tested against a completely different screenshot (different match, same 3 cards) — that second result is the one that actually proves the approach generalizes, not the trivial self-match one.

CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD = 0.85 in card_detector.py: below this, a slot reports UNKNOWN instead of a wrong best-guess. Validated against a real uncovered card (Giant, before it was added) scoring 0.470 — comfortably below threshold, confirming the cutoff correctly separates real matches from noise.

Red/blue channel-swap bug found and fixed (2026-08-15): perception/capture.py's screenshot() previously returned RGB from PIL directly, but every cv2-based script in the project (card_detector, etc.) assumed BGR — the root cause of early templates looking "blue and yellow" instead of true colors. Fixed by converting RGB→BGR in capture.py itself. One-time repair tools: perception/fix_color_channels.py (fixed the channel order of every pre-fix file in place — a reversible swap, no data lost) and perception/cleanup_pre_fix_files.py (deleted the small set of pre-fix files that weren't safely repairable this way: literal seed.png files from migrate_templates.py's very first run, plus everything that was sitting in the unlabeled queue at the time — see that script's docstring for why those specifically couldn't just be channel-swapped back). Both are one-time/historical now; nothing currently in the repo is still affected.

Template storage restructured (perception/migrate_templates.py, one-time): assets/hand_templates/<card_name>/ now holds MULTIPLE example images per card instead of one frozen file, since matching against many real examples (different animation moments, lighting, the low-elixir grey state) is what makes matching robust — not a single frozen reference. Files are stored as true single-channel grayscale (not color converted-to-look-grayscale), which also structurally rules out the channel-swap bug recurring.

Semi-automated data collection pipeline added (2026-08-15): perception/auto_collect.py runs continuously during a live match, auto-filing high-confidence hand crops as new examples (deduped against near-duplicates, capped at MAX_EXAMPLES_PER_CARD=40 real examples/card) and queuing low-confidence crops to assets/unlabeled_queue/ instead of interrupting play. perception/review_queue.py batch-labels that queue afterward. perception/debug_live.py gives a live color-coded overlay (green=confident, orange=weak guess, red=unknown) so bad recognition is visible in real time without reading terminal output — safe to run alongside auto_collect.py in a second terminal. perception/collect_cards.py is the older one-shot manual alternative (capture once, prompt for any UNKNOWN slot) — still there for quick top-ups when not running a full auto_collect session.

Deck-restricted matching added: config/deck.txt lists the current 8-card deck (one name per line); when present, auto_collect.py and debug_live.py restrict matching to just those 8 cards instead of the full template library — more accurate (no false match against an unrelated card) and any recognition failure is known for certain to be an under-represented example of one of those 8. perception/seed_deck_from_catalog.py bootstraps any deck card with zero real examples yet from its Supercell catalog icon (weak match on its own, but enough to steer the first real occurrence toward the right guess when matching is restricted to only 8 cards) — run this once per deck change, before the first collection session.

Coverage as of 2026-08-15 (8 cards, all from the active deck in config/deck.txt, in assets/hand_templates/): archers (13), arrows (21), fireball (22), giant (21), knight (15), mini_pekka (21), minions (11), musketeer (26) — real examples, built up via auto_collect.py during actual play, well beyond the earlier single-example-per-card state. Unlabeled queue is currently empty (nothing awaiting review). download_card_icons.py's output in assets/card_icons/ (122 catalog icons) is used now only as seed_deck_from_catalog.py's fallback source, not for direct matching.

How to add more cards: get into any match, python -m perception.auto_collect for hands-off collection (recommended), or python -m perception.label_hand <name1> <name2> <name3> <name4> / collect_cards.py for a manual one-shot. Coverage grows opportunistically as more matches are played — no need to force covering all 122 up front.

Known unhandled edge case: cards you can't afford yet are dimmed/ grayed out in the live hand UI. That will look visually different from a full-elixir capture of the same card and will likely fail to match at high confidence — not yet tested or handled. Will need either a second "dimmed" template per card, or a normalization step (e.g. brightness/ saturation correction) before matching. Revisit when it actually causes a real missed detection, rather than solving preemptively.

Elixir counting — status: DONE, verified live against real gameplay (2026-08-15)

Files: perception/elixir_counter.py (both signals + combining logic), perception/label_elixir.py (adds a new value to the template library from the current test_screenshot.png, same pattern as label_hand.py).

Design, refined from the original roadmap plan: NOT pure pixel counting alone (the original plan) — combines two signals. The NUMBER readout (white digit(s) over the droplet, one of 11 possible values 0-10) is template-matched the same way card_detector.py matches cards, and is the trusted signal for the integer part. The 10-segment BAR next to it is measured by pixel fill (algorithmic, no templates needed) for the FRACTIONAL progress toward the next point — confirmed genuinely continuous (not a discrete per-segment indicator) by comparing two live captures ~15s apart during calibration and seeing the partial-fill width shrink proportionally, consistent with real elixir regen rather than a fixed "next up" highlight.

Bar geometry (BAR_LEFT_X=244.5, BAR_RIGHT_X=864.0, 10 equal segments) calibrated against a real 900x1600 capture by locating the segment tick-divider lines pixel-by-pixel — positions were identical across independent captures, so treated as fixed. Re-calibrate (same as hand_region.py) if LDPlayer's resolution setting ever changes.

Real bug found and fixed during calibration: the elixir NUMBER glyph is drawn directly on top of the bar's own leftmost ~90px (overlapping segments 1-2), so a naive left-to-right pixel scan misread the digit's white/black pixels as bar fill — harmless when true fill already extended past that point, but badly wrong at low elixir (a true reading of 0 came back as 0.66). Two-part fix: (1) classify "filled" pixels using sum(R,G,B) > threshold AND green channel < 200, since both real fill colors (solid pink, and the lighter in-progress glow) have low green while the white digit text doesn't; (2) scan the bar right-to-left with per-row majority voting (not averaging raw pixel colors across sample rows first, which was blending digit black/white into false-positive intermediate colors) — scanning from the right confirms real fill from the unambiguous side of the bar, clear of the digit overlay entirely except at very low elixir. Verified against real captures after the fix: true 0 → 0.11, true 3 → 3.32, true 4 → 4.65-4.66, true 10 (2x-elixir mode) → 10.00.

Digit template coverage: full, all 11 values (0-10) now have at least one real example in assets/elixir_templates/ after a single match run with auto_collect.py's self-labeling (2026-08-15) — some values (1, 5, 7) picked up several examples in that one match. A few landed in the wrong value's folder initially (self-labeling is bar-estimate-based, and the bar can briefly misread during transition animations — see limitation below); Duncan spotted and manually moved these, and no fix was needed beyond that.

Verified live via debug_live.py (2026-08-15): reads correctly during normal play. The one known gap is transition states — while a card is actively being selected/placed, the bar/number readout is briefly unreliable (mid-animation). Deliberately not solving this at the perception layer: once cycle-tracking and known elixir costs exist (Phase 4), the system can cross-check/bridge these brief gaps from game logic (a played card's known cost + timing) rather than needing pixel-perfect reads through every animation frame. Revisit only if this actually causes a downstream problem.

Auto-collection added (2026-08-15): auto_collect.py's existing per-match capture loop (originally cards-only) now also self-labels elixir digit examples in the same loop, no extra ADB calls. Manually typing python -m perception.label_elixir <value> with the right timing turned out to be impractical during real play (by the time you've read the value and typed the command, the value's often already changed) — auto-collection removes the timing problem entirely. Unlike cards, elixir can't bootstrap off its own template-match confidence (plenty of values may have zero templates on day one), so it self-labels off elixir_counter.py's BAR reading instead, which needs no templates at all: a frame is only auto-filed when the bar's continuous estimate is within ELIXIR_STABLE_MARGIN=0.12 of a whole number (i.e. we just ticked over to that value, not mid-regen), making the inferred integer label trustworthy even before any digit template exists for it. Much shorter tail than cards (11 possible values, most seen within a game or two), so there's no separate review-queue step — anything ambiguous is just skipped, not queued. python -m perception.label_elixir <value> still exists as a manual fallback but shouldn't normally be needed now.

Known limitation, not yet handled: no "are we actually in a match" check — running the reader against a menu/transition screen (e.g. the post-match results screen) produces a plausible-looking but meaningless number instead of failing loudly, since the digit-match confidence is low but the bar-fill measurement doesn't know it's looking at nonsense either. Not a problem for the perception module itself (Phase 5's scripted bot will presumably already know match-state from elsewhere), but worth a guard if elixir_counter.py is ever called standalone.

